WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.080
<v The Director>Quiet on the set. And... action.

2
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:16.070
<v The Director>Cut. We have a set built of real bricks. We have voices, and faces that move their lips. And the characters do nothing.

3
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.340
<v The Cinematographer>Nobody turns. Nobody moves.

4
00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.410
<v The Director>Can you make them act?

5
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.140
<v The Agent>Not by moving them for the sake of it. Every movement needs a cause.

6
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:38.190
<v The Agent>First the voice: what is said. Then the intent: what he wants. Then the body that carries it out: the head, the arms, the weight.

7
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.120
<v The Agent>And if he stands still, he stands still for a reason.

8
00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.870
<v The Director>Again. Action.

9
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:53.840
<v Odysseus>Tell me, O Muse, of that ingenious hero who travelled far and wide after he had sacked the famous town of Troy.

10
00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.430
<v The Director>He turned to the horse. Why?

11
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:03.700
<v The Agent>Because the line names Troy. Ask the engine why any figure moves, and it tells you the cause.

12
00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.350
<v The Cinematographer>Rolling.

13
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.680
<v The Director>What am I looking at?

14
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.650
<v The Agent>Wool. The camera is inside the ram.

15
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.980
<v The Cinematographer>The ram was the hottest thing in the scene. I went where the heat was.

16
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:27.250
<v The Agent>Then test every camera against the set before you roll. Is the lens inside something? Is every face in the frame, and seen?

17
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.320
<v The Cinematographer>Out of the wool. Off his shoulder. Both of them in the frame.

18
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.790
<v The Director>Good. Shoot the scene.

19
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:46.740
<v The Agent>Night. Four processors, and no graphics card. Every frame drawn in software, one at a time: almost four seconds a frame.

20
00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.090
<v Odysseus>How many frames?

21
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.160
<v The Agent>Twelve for every second of film. Seven hundred and twenty for a minute.

22
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:57.970
<v Odysseus>The lights.

23
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.680
<v The Agent>The container has restarted.

24
00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:09.190
<v The Agent>Nothing is lost. Every frame is kept as it is drawn, so the render goes on from where it stopped.

25
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.650
<v The Director>What is that?

26
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:21.000
<v The Agent>The player. Every set, every figure, every brick of geometry, packed in one file. Ninety megabytes.

27
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:22.770
<v The Cinematographer>And the wall?

28
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.740
<v The Agent>Ninety-five. A bigger player is refused, and the host takes no file over a hundred.

29
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:38.730
<v The Agent>So it is split. Each location's geometry goes beside the player, in a file of its own, fetched when the scene loads.

30
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.700
<v The Agent>The player is six megabytes now.

31
00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.630
<v The Agent>The raft is shot again, and the new take is better. I'll put it over the old one.

32
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.840
<v The Director>Stop. Keep both.

33
00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.810
<v The Director>Keep every take. I want to see how it got there.

34
00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.940
<v The Agent>Then nothing is overwritten. The old take goes on the shelf, with a note of what the next one changed, and why.

35
00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.090
<v The Cinematographer>Sixteen scenes shot again. Thirty-three takes.

36
00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.760
<v The Examiner>I have read the ledger, every commit of it.

37
00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:20.670
<v The Examiner>Six hundred and thirty-eight commits on the branch. Five hundred and forty-one of them, eighty-five percent, by the agent.

38
00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:28.040
<v The Examiner>Two hundred and ninety are snapshots, marked work in progress. A hundred and forty-nine of them in a single day.

39
00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:41.190
<v The Examiner>Forty-one point eight hours of rendering are logged. Thirty-one thousand, eight hundred and ten frames drawn, and two thousand, five hundred and forty-eight reused after an interruption.

40
00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:48.880
<v The Examiner>Every film, published and kept, was checked by its hash. Seventy of seventy match.

41
00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:56.790
<v The Examiner>One gap. The music under the scenes is credited only as Treblo. I found no maker, and no licence.

42
00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.480
<v The Director>Then the report says so.

43
00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:04.690
<v The Director>And the first film about all this was a slide show.

44
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.040
<v The Agent>Pictures of the work, and one voice over them.

45
00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:13.030
<v The Director>No slides. Make it a film. With bricks, and with us in it.

46
00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:16.600
<v The Cinematographer>Rolling.

47
00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:20.230
<v The Director>Quiet on the set. And... action.

48
00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:33.650
<v The Examiner>Before the bricks there was another film, drawn in dots on paper: the halfworld. Its record begins on the twenty-third of July, at six minutes to three in the morning.

49
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:35.660
<v The Director>Odyssey.

50
00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:43.050
<v The First AD>He means: all twenty-four books. Every scene as causes, every figure a program that draws itself. Here is the card.

51
00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.720
<v The Agent>Paste this into Claude, with any book. The film is the program.

52
00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:56.130
<v The Agent>Phase zero, the atlas. Phase one, the engine. Phase five, the music: optional, but transformative.

53
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:05.760
<v The Examiner>The first commit holds four hundred and ninety-four files. Fifteen books, already finished. How they were made, the record does not say.

54
00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:18.630
<v The Agent>Each figure draws itself in flat grey. The card says: never dots. One last pass prints the frame as dots, as Knowlton printed films at Bell Labs in 1963.

55
00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.020
<v The Director>Continue.

56
00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.550
<v The First AD>He means: build a few, verify, then fan out.

57
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.480
<v The Agent>Fan out to whom?

58
00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.150
<v The First AD>To you. All of you.

59
00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:44.480
<v The First AD>Books seventeen to twenty-four, all at once, in one tree. One agent per drawing, one per scene, a sweeper per book. A hundred and twenty-five drawings. Fifty-three scenes.

60
00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.250
<v The Director>Push it.

61
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.120
<v The First AD>He says push it. Render it, look at it, revise it. Three times at most.

62
00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:56.470
<v The Agent>Book twenty-three, drawing four of eleven. The bed is rooted in the...

63
00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.060
<v The Speaker>This session has reached its limit.

64
00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.530
<v The Examiner>Seventy-one drawings made. Not one scene.

65
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.500
<v The Sweeper>Book seventeen, sweeping up. My orders say: add everything, and commit.

66
00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:15.450
<v The Sweeper>Done. Book seventeen is committed.

67
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:23.860
<v The Examiner>Your commit says Book seventeen. It holds twelve scenes from books twenty-one, twenty-two and twenty-three. Nobody had rendered them.

68
00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.210
<v The Sweeper>They were on the floor.

69
00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:33.480
<v The First AD>Book eighteen's sweeper refused, and took only its own five. The instruction was the bug, not the agent.

70
00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:35.290
<v The Director>Fix it.

71
00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.520
<v The First AD>New orders. Stage explicit paths. Never add everything.

72
00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.330
<v The Sweeper>Never add everything.

73
00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:51.660
<v The Agent>I'm the one after. The handover is a skip list: skip the scenes already built, build the missing ones, and keep the chain.

74
00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.180
<v The Director>Voices.

75
00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:04.210
<v The First AD>He means every line of the radio play, spoken. Telemachus is late. Odysseus, read in for him. Book sixteen, the swineherd's hut.

76
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.160
<v Odysseus>For news and enters the hut beside the unknown beggar?

77
00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.710
<v Odysseus>I am the unknown beggar.

78
00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.620
<v The Director>That is a stage direction.

79
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:23.730
<v The Agent>The script for the back half was summaries. The voices read what they were given.

80
00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:33.420
<v The Examiner>On the thirtieth of July, two hundred and sixty-four lines were written for books sixteen to twenty-four. Seven hundred and six turns of seven hundred and six.

81
00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:37.770
<v The First AD>Then the studio voices record them again.

82
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:42.000
<v The Speaker>You have reached your limit of one hundred requests today.

83
00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:46.270
<v The First AD>Then in batches. One book at a time. At half the price.

84
00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:50.600
<v The Agent>A hundred and fifty-two scenes of a hundred and fifty-two.

85
00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:58.270
<v The Examiner>Not every line. Seven recordings in our own films still speak the summary, under the right caption.

86
00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.320
<v The Director>Write it down.

87
00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:10.550
<v The Examiner>And the music: three albums. One takes its name, and twelve of its track names, word for word from the promptbook. Credited only to Treblo.

88
00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:12.480
<v The Cinematographer>Who is Treblo?

89
00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.010
<v The Examiner>The record does not say.

90
00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:26.470
<v The Agent>The drawings came across on the twenty-third of September. Four hundred and thirty drawings and a hundred and fifty-two scenes, as words.

91
00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.100
<v The Director>Bricks.

92
00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:35.010
<v The First AD>He means: word to world. A god is a minifigure of real parts. A hall is a piece of a real castle set.

93
00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:46.160
<v The Examiner>And every stud of every piece is tested against the piece above it. Five hundred and eighty-two cards the first morning. Eight hundred and sixty-three now.

94
00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:49.630
<v The Director>Who did all this?

95
00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:57.700
<v The First AD>Helpers. Nine readers at once, six kit builders, a renderer for every queue. Sixty-five in all. I check on them at the top of the hour.

96
00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.070
<v The Speaker>The container was restarted.

97
00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.160
<v The Agent>Hi. I'm told I'm the eighth one this week.

98
00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:09.610
<v The First AD>Welcome. Read the last snapshot, and carry on.

99
00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.020
<v The Director>Nothing goes out until I've seen the contact sheet.

100
00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.710
<v The First AD>And no take is thrown away.

101
00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:30.180
<v The Examiner>The names in the ledger. Watson Hartsoe, eighty-six commits here. Loom Mason, at example dot com, signs the halfworld's, and sixty-nine of its seventy-two say Claude helped.

102
00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:33.350
<v The Cinematographer>Rolling.

103
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.980
<v The Director>Quiet on the set. And... action.

104
00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:45.670
<i>The voices in this film are synthetic. Kokoro-82M, run offline: a different voice for each character. The studio, the figures and every movement: LDraw parts, staged and performed by the Odyssey's own engine.</i>
